This is the README for GNU awk 5 in the WSL
(Windows Subsystem for Linux) environment of MS-Windows

* A normal Linux install
------------------------

    In itself, installing gawk in a WSL environment is just a normal
Linux install, once the required libraries are in place.

    What follows has been tested using an Ubuntu WSL distribution,
but should work also for other WSL distributions.


* Getting the required libraries
--------------------------------

    Starting from a vanilla Linux distribution, the following
commands should be given before running the commands to perform
the Gawk installation:

# To get the latest updates for the just installed distribution:

	sudo apt update
	sudo apt upgrade

# To get the tools needed to compile gawk:

	sudo apt install gcc
	sudo apt install make

# To get the locales needed to check the locale capabilities of gawk.
# This is not really necessary, and not all the locales listed below may be
# available. If not, the locale tests will just be skipped.

	sudo apt install language-pack-en
	sudo apt install language-pack-fr
	sudo apt install language-pack-ja
	sudo apt install language-pack-ru

# If the (now deprecated) MPFR capabilities
# (Arbitrary Precision Arithmetic) are needed.

	sudo apt install libmpfr-dev libgmp-dev

# Not really necessary, but if not installed
# a few tests will not be run.

	sudo apt install libreadline-dev


* Building gawk
---------------

    Download the distribution file.

    Give the usual commands:

	tar -xvpzf gawk-5.4.x.tar.gz
	cd gawk-5.4.x
	./configure && make

    After that, the installation tests may be run giving:

	make test

...but see the Caveat below.

One does not need to use "sudo" for a personal installation,
but should be able to become super-user to install the build.
The installed version goes into /usr/local/bin, which takes precedence
over the version of gawk that comes with WSL, and resides in /usr/bin.

	sudo make install

* Caveat
--------

    In itself, gawk can be built everywhere, i.e., inside or outside
the WSL Linux file System.

    The command "make check" in the WSL environment can give different
results if the gawk executable is inside the Linux file system, or
outside it.  Since outside the Linux file system the "inodes" are
simulated, and not "the real thing", a few tests can fail (and their
failure is ignored).  Inside the Linux file system (e.g., in a home
directory), all the tests should succeed.

    Also, the command "make check" should be executed by a "normal"
user, and not by "root".  This is because the PMA feature, for security
reasons, is not available (and thus, cannot be tested) by the user "root".
